entries.html:
To validate data received via the Mini App, one should send the data from the <i>Telegram.WebApp.initData</i> field to the bot's backend. The data is a query string, which is composed of a series of field-value pairs.

You can verify the integrity of the data received by comparing the received <i>hash</i> parameter with the hexadecimal representation of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash-based_message_authentication_code">HMAC-SHA-256</a> signature of the <b>data-check-string</b> with the secret key, which is the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash-based_message_authentication_code">HMAC-SHA-256</a> signature of the <a href="https://core.telegram.org/bots/tutorial#obtain-your-bot-token">bot's token</a> with the constant string <code>WebAppData</code> used as a key.

<b>Data-check-string</b> is a chain of all received fields, sorted alphabetically, in the format <code>key=&lt;value&gt;</code> with a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newline">line feed</a> character ('
', 0x0A) used as separator – e.g., <code>'auth_date=&lt;auth_date&gt;
query_id=&lt;query_id&gt;
user=&lt;user&gt;'</code>.

The full check might look like:

<pre><code>data_check_string = ...
secret_key = HMAC_SHA256(&lt;bot_token&gt;, "WebAppData")
if (hex(HMAC_SHA256(data_check_string, secret_key)) == hash) {
  // data is from Telegram
}</code></pre>

To prevent the use of outdated data, you can additionally check the <i>auth_date</i> field, which contains a Unix timestamp of when it was received by the Mini App.

Once validated, the data may be used on your server. Complex data types are represented as JSON-serialized objects.
